/* CHIRATA ANDREI 313 CA

###--MAIN--###

I have organised my project such that in main I have a loop that reads the
commands one by one. I used strstr function to recognize the command and then
call the corresponding function.

###--DATA STRUCTS FUNCTIONS--##

In structs.h, I wrote the definiton for every struct I used: doubly linked
list, stack, queue, and also other structs for the project. In data_struct
functions.h, I implemented the headers for the data structures functions 
(basic functions that adds / removes an element, etc). And in data_struct
functions.c, I implemented the functions as I did in labs, with some new things
like the sentinel node.

###--OPERATIONS--###

In operations.h, there are the headers for the functions of the program that
are called from main and their implementation is in operations.c.

First function, init_system allocs dynamic all the structures for the project
logic. Also, it initializes the sentinel nodes with the values from the tasks.

The add incident function extracts data from input and adds the incident to
the list and it also adds it to the corresponding queue, by priority. I use
strtok function to extract the words one by one and find out the priority.
To add the node, I use data struct functions.

The dispatch function finds the incident with the biggest priority, then marks
its status intervened. Also takes the first unit from the units queue using
dequeue function and pairs the incident and the unit into a new intervention.

The undo_last_dipatch function finds the last incident that has been dispatched
using the history stack and adds it to the queue by its priority, using enqueue
function. And then, it deletes the node from the intervention list.

Then, the solved_incident function finds an incident with a specific id and 
marks it solved.

Then there are two funtions: show_unit, show_incident, that prints the data
with a specific id. Also, show_interventions function prints all the interven
tions.

Finally, there is free_all function that calls all the free data struct 
functions, freeing all the allocated memory.

*/